{
  "description": "#5071 — the two renders of the markdown grammar, each over the same page: the export and a clipboard copy (the page's buffer is pinned line by line through `get_page_buffer`). A document cannot ride in a row token (`#` is the attribute separator, and every export opens `# Title`), so each step's row names the sibling file beside this one that holds the backend's render, `markdown_documents.<step>.txt`, with the blake3 of its bytes; `CONFORMANCE_UPDATE=1` writes the file and the Rust runner asserts the render equals it. The grammar has one implementation, in Rust: the mock's renders are deliberate approximations, so this fixture's query leg is backend-only on the mock (`QUERY_DRIFT_SKIP` in conformance.test.ts). One page carries the shapes that matter: a heading, nesting two deep, a block whose text runs over two lines, inline marks, a same-page block ref (the export anchors its target), a page link and a tag ref (written as names), page front matter (a property, an alias and a tag), and a block attachment and a page attachment (the export writes a link line for each). Seed: S1 (Field Notes) > S2 (## Findings) > S3 (inline marks) > S4; S1 > S5 (two lines), S6, S7; S8 (Reading List); S9 (Tags) > S10 (research). The ops give S6 its ref to S3 and S7 its link to S8 and tag S10 through `edit_block`, because the seed insert bypasses the materializer that derives `block_links`. Re-author `expected` / `expected_queries` and the documents via CONFORMANCE_UPDATE=1.",
